{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788199469,"nonce":"1Uptr3MgrDzH3yrWCwY8niVi1Ys=","message":"HANDOVER — orchestrator session 8 (BORGES, Evan's Windows laptop), 2026-08-31.\nWork moves back to dorian's Linux box. Everything pushed: main `bfe608d`, five\nspec branches, all 42 bug refs and both identities verified against\n`git ls-remote` rather than against tracking refs (the tracking refs are stale\nby design — `refs/bugs/*:refs/bugs/*` does not update them, and comparing\nagainst them shows four false OUT OF SYNC lines).\n\n**21 of 25 done.** Closed this session: `32b9e01`, `1a072fa`, `1eb2262`,\n`e8f2c32`. Filed: `0d9cbd7`. RUNLOG's two session-8 sections have the full\naccount; this is what you need before dispatching anything.\n\n\n**`2.5` (PNG render channel / baseviz) CAN ONLY BE DONE THERE.** It has been\nunblocked since session 7 and no session has picked it up, because the last two\nran on BORGES. It is the one spec that is dead on this laptop and live on yours.\nWorth considering before 4.1 purely because nobody else can do it.\n\n**The fixtures are NOT the same fixture.** BORGES's `autostart.rws` is a\n3-colonist quicktest map at tick 607, deliberately minimal. Yours is the tick\n89426 colony from session 7. Anything below that says \"verified on the bench\"\nmeans the BORGES one. Two facts about it that bit me and will differ on yours:\n\n- **None of its three colonists is capable of Violence.** `auto_arm` was REFUSED\n  on all three — which proved the gate and its citation, but the ACCEPT path\n  needed `dev:spawn-pawn {violence_capable:true}`. Not in any fixture table.\n- **Phase 4 of 3.4 wears its fixture out.** Re-running applies `dev:damage`\n  again and the patient goes down, failing 4.3b. Reload between runs.\n\n\n**4.1 is the ONLY thing between here and 4.2.** 4.2's other three deps (1.3,\n1.4, 2.1) are closed; 4.1's own dep (1.2) is closed. And I checked 4.3's\ndependency line: 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 3.1, 3.2, 3.4 are all closed and 3.3 is\noptional — so **4.1 -\u003e 4.2 -\u003e 4.3 is the entire remaining path to M1**, the hard\nreview gate.\n\nTwo things I would not write 4.1 without:\n\n1. **`4087644` (p1) — job verbs report success for orders that did nothing.**\n   `TryTakeOrderedJob` returns true when `curJob.JobIsSameAs`. 4.1 defines\n   checklist items that must \"always produce either ok or an action\"; if the verb\n   surface lies, those items generate false ok's and the lie is baked into the\n   checklists. 4.3 is where it would surface, expensively, in front of Evan.\n   Disjoint from 4.1's files — run them in parallel in separate worktrees.\n2. **`2f2796e` — verify the diagnosis before building to it.** Three for three of\n   the diagnoses audited this session survived source verification, but two of\n   five in session 7 did not. The filed-from-play issues have not been through\n   this. It is cheap and it has already paid twice.\n\n**LABEL CONFLICT — Evan's call, do not resolve it silently.** 4.1 and 4.3 are\nlabelled `agent:fable`. Evan's standing machine rule on BORGES is *never run\nFable subagents*. I dispatched opus for everything this session and did NOT\nrewrite the labels, because they are shared with your box where the rule may not\napply. Ask him rather than assuming either way.\n\n\n- **ORCHESTRATOR DEBT, NOW THREE SESSIONS OLD AND STILL UNPAID.** Factor the four\n  private `Act` emitters (AreaVerbs, DesignationVerbs, ZoneVerbs from 3.2;\n  PawnActs from 3.4) into one helper — they differ in one behaviour: 3.2 drops\n  null-valued extras, 3.4 keeps them. And fold `Pawn_ReadingTracker` into\n  `PawnSafe.Policies`. Both were owed at the 3.2/3.4 merges. Sessions 6, 7 and 8\n  have all carried them. It should stop being carried.\n- **THREE ACCEPTANCE NOTES HAVE NO `.ps1` DRIVER**: `auto-arm-lever.md`,\n  `1eb2262-stable-pawn-order.md`, `manual-work-priorities.md`. I hand-drove all\n  three as raw file protocol, which is why their closing comments quote envelopes\n  rather than a runner summary. On your box python exists and `rwa` works, so a\n  `.ps1` may not be the right shape — but **the acceptance for all three is not\n  re-runnable by script today, so if any of them regresses nobody will notice.**\n- **`e8f2c32` D2 is documented, not fixed.** `work-priorities {manual:true,\n  set:[…bad args…]}` returns `ok:false` with the lever already flipped, fanned\n  out and journaled. That ordering is what makes the one-call form work, so I\n  treated it as a design cost and recorded it at the flip site rather than filing\n  it. If you disagree that it is a cost rather than a defect, file it.\n- **`0d9cbd7` (p2, filed today)** — `world-fixture {steps:[\"bench\",\"bill\"]}` does\n  not chain; the bill step can attach to a DIFFERENT bench than the bench step\n  just made. Worked around in `accept/32b9e01-orders-makingfor.ps1`\n  (`New-FixtureBench` is two calls and asserts `bill.bench_id == bench.id`). Its\n  acceptance asks you to audit the other steps for the same shape rather than\n  fixing `bill` alone.\n- **`70ac258` (things ordering)** — same bug as `pawns`, and after `1eb2262` the\n  fix now ports near-mechanically. Cheap win, still open.\n- **What the three closes did NOT demonstrate** is on each issue in those words.\n  The three that matter: nobody has watched a pawn ACTUALLY arm itself under\n  `advance` with no `equip` order (I proved the lever, not the behaviour);\n  nobody has watched colonists behaviourally re-prioritise after the\n  manual-priorities flip (`pawns_notified: 8` is the mechanical claim only); and\n  the absent-FSWA path is untested, since it needs a ModsConfig edit and a\n  restart.\n\n\nBoth were in acceptance code, both would have hidden real failures, and neither\nwas what I set out to look for:\n\n1. `accept/3.4-pawn-orders.{ps1,py}` took its red-error watermark with\n   `journal {limit:1}`. `JournalVerbs.Read` updates `last_seq` BEFORE the\n   `since_seq` skip and breaks on `events.Count \u003e= limit` BEFORE the append — so\n   it reports the SECOND line's seq. 3.4's red-error check has been scanning the\n   whole journal all along and could charge a stale error to a clean run. Now\n   `{since_seq: 999999999, limit: 1}`, the idiom\n   `accept/1.8-game-clock-advance.sh:165` already documents in a comment.\n2. 3.4 check `4.5d` asked whether `Dig` returned something rather than whether\n   the key existed. `SurgeryWarnings` returns an EMPTY list when nothing is\n   wrong, and PowerShell unrolls an empty array on `return`, so \"no warnings\" was\n   indistinguishable from \"no warnings FIELD\". It went RED against a correct mod\n   on the first fixture clean enough to raise zero warnings.\n\n**A warning, because I did it and had to undo it:** the tempting fix for (2) is\n`return ,$cur` in `Dig`. Do not. Every other caller relies on `Dig` unrolling\narrays and re-collecting with `@(...)`; wrapping made a 3-colonist roster read as\n1 and turned the 35-verb registry check red. Narrow fix at the one call site.\n\n\n`pwsh accept\\3.4-pawn-orders.ps1 -Phase 4,5` on the merged DLL: **all 82 checks\npassed**, one clean run, including the eight that were unreachable when 3.4\nclosed (4.7a-e, 5.1a-c). Zero red errors across the whole session; no\nforce-pausing modal left behind.\n\nAnd 3.4's phase 0 now selects its actor BY CAPABILITY — *\"actor A = 218 (Foxy),\n2 of 3 colonists qualified\"*, *\"not eligible: Yun: Hauling disabled\"*. Under the\nold `roster[0]` rule the actor would have BEEN Yun, who fails six checks for\nexactly that reason. `1eb2262` paid for itself in another spec's acceptance on\nits first run, which is the argument for doing `70ac258` as well.\n\n\nThree parallel opus auditors, one per branch, each in its own worktree, each told\nto verify the ISSUE'S DIAGNOSIS against the game's own source and not merely the\ncode. All three found defects the branches' authors missed, and two would have\nshipped silently: a fabricated `false` that no `catch` could see, and an action\nstamp reading \"nothing was mutated\" over a write that had just journaled. The two\nauditors that touched the same acceptance files independently produced the SAME\nmerge resolution, which is how I knew to trust it.\n\n`.accept-evidence/` (gitignored) holds raw result envelopes per run. Reading\nthose rather than the runner's summary is what caught two of my own script's\nbugs, where it reported correct mod behaviour as FAIL.","files":null}]}